  1464	**6** **RELATED WORK**
  1465	
  1466	
  1467	Inferring a minimal automaton with a given number of states and
  1468	consistent with a set of traces was shown to be NP-hard by Gold [ 16 ].
  1469	Nevertheless, there exists a variety of inference approaches for different types of automata using, among others, CSP, SAT and SMT
  1470	solving: The problem was originally stated by Biermann and Feldman [ 9 ] as a CSP problem, which was later formulated as a SAT
  1471	problem by Grinchtein et al. [ 17 ]. Their approach was improved by
  1472	Heule and Verwer [ 19 ] by combining exact SAT solving with greedy
  1473	state-merging and heuristics for model inference. Avellaneda and
  1474	Petrenko [ 5 ] present an incremental approach especially for inferring automata from long traces. Neider [ 39 ] proposes a technique
  1475	to infer deterministic finite automata (DFAs) with a fixed number of
  1476	states. Smetsers et al. [ 46 ] infer DFAs, Mealy machines, and register
  1477	automata from observed behaviour using SMT solving. Tappler et
  1478	al. [47] uses SMT solving to learn timed automata.
